{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf46Ww8suYgtTc1DhcNYX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Current state first\n\nThe rate-limit initiative is **proposed and reviewed, not executed**. Evidence:\n\n- `tasks/.plans/rate-limit/delivery-plan.yaml:2` — `status: DELIVERY_PLAN=READY`\n- `reviews/delivery-plan-review.json` — `disposition: accepted`, reviewer `fixture-controller`, HMAC plan-review signature, and an explicit reason: *\"no leaf execution authorization\"*\n- `task-plan.json` compiled with `approved: true`, 4 units\n- `tasks/` holds only `.gitkeep`; `taskspec ready` returns an empty queue → **no leaves materialized, sealed, run, or accepted**\n\nSo everything below is plan-level topology, not implementation proof.\n\n## Why these are four tasks, not one\n\nEach leaf owns one outcome, one authorization boundary, one write surface, and one independently assessable completion. All four axes differ:\n\n| Task | Seam / owner (authority) | Write surface (`creates_paths`) | Independently assessable completion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `T-20260802-policy-schema` | SEAM-POLICY-CONTRACT / `platform-contracts` | `src/rate_limit/policy.schema.json`, `tests/test_policy_schema.py` | Schema accepts valid limits/windows, rejects non-positive (terminal `eval_3`) |\n| `T-20260802-effective-policy` | SEAM-POLICY-RESOLUTION / `policy-runtime` | `src/rate_limit/policy.py`, `tests/test_policy_resolution.py` | One stable policy + provenance per input (terminal `eval_3`) |\n| `T-20260802-request-101` | SEAM-REQUEST-ENFORCEMENT / `request-runtime` | `src/rate_limit/enforcement.py`, `tests/test_request_101.py` | 1–100 allowed, 101 denied once, no off-by-one (terminal `eval_3`) |\n| `T-20260802-decision-visibility` | SEAM-DECISION-VISIBILITY / `reliability-interface` | `src/rate_limit/decision.py`, `tests/test_decision_visibility.py` | Response and telemetry share reason + decision id (terminal `eval_3`) |\n\nFour distinct owners, four disjoint create sets, and `touches_paths: []` on every unit — the work is purely additive with no shared write surface and no contentions (`proposed-graph.json: \"contentions\": []`).\n\nThe seam files record *why* each boundary was cut, as rejected alternatives — this is the strongest separation evidence in the plan:\n\n- Policy contract: rejected *\"treat the API handler as the policy boundary\"* — would mix contract, resolution, and enforcement concerns.\n- Policy resolution: rejected *\"resolve policy independently inside every request adapter\"* — multiple precedence implementations would drift and become unprovable.\n- Request enforcement: rejected *\"infer enforcement from emitted metrics\"* — telemetry observes a decision but cannot authorize or make it.\n- Decision visibility: rejected *\"emit a metric without returning a stable reason\"* — callers could not distinguish limit denial from unrelated failure.\n\nEnforcement and visibility are the subtlest split, and the plan defends it twice: the anti-pattern *\"do not let telemetry authorize the denial\"* sits in the visibility unit, and *\"do not use telemetry as the enforcement source\"* sits in the enforcement unit. Same boundary, guarded from both sides.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`proposed-graph.json` declares three `depends_on` edges forming a single linear critical path, matching `steel-thread.md` and `ADR-RATE-LIMIT-ORDER` (accepted):\n\n```\npolicy-schema → effective-policy → request-101 → decision-visibility\n```\n\nThe data contract behind each edge comes from the leg `requires`/`produces` pairs:\n\n- schema **produces** `validated policy document` → resolution **requires** it\n- resolution **produces** `effective policy decision` → enforcement **requires** it\n- enforcement **produces** `allow-or-deny decision` → visibility **requires** it\n\nOne caution on reading this: separate does not mean input-independent. These four are independently *assessable* — each has its own terminal eval and its own rollback — but each downstream leaf still needs its declared dependency satisfied to produce its proof. The chain is fully serial; there is no parallelism to gain here. The separation buys distinct ownership, bounded blast radius, and provable completion per boundary, not concurrency.\n\n## Two boundary facts worth carrying forward\n\n- **Metering is deliberately outside the thread.** `OBJ-METERING-SEPARATION` is recorded as `FIXED` in both `system-map.md` and `delivery-plan.yaml`, and `intent.md` lists *\"replacing provider-owned usage metering\"* and production storage/deployment choices as out of scope. Window/counter state is an external dependency supplied by an implementation-selected adapter.\n- **No integration-proof leaf exists.** No unit declares `proves_capabilities`. Each leg proves itself, but nothing in this plan independently proves the end-to-end path from an authored policy document through to a denial with matching telemetry. Per the decomposition guide, a capability with no proof leaf stays unproven even when all children are done. That's a gap in the plan as written, not a blocker on the current state — worth deciding before you seal leaves.\n\n## Smallest next action\n\nNothing is authorized to execute. If you want to proceed, the next command is:\n\n```bash\ntaskspec batch --plan tasks/.plans/rate-limit/task-plan.json\n```\n\nThat creates the four **unsealed** leaf scaffolds — it does not authorize execution. Sealing their exact revision requires a separate `gate --stamp`, and verified completion requires `accept`. I haven't run it; say the word if you want the leaves materialized."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4776,"cache_read_input_tokens":35854,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4776},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14a2f32c-9fea-4795-8643-1c0e730b4775","uuid":"7e63d46c-9522-41de-a8ad-f0be9f8e1320","timestamp":"2026-09-14T22:10:10.023Z","request_id":"req_011Cf46WvcsnVd5h1ioQs5sz"}
